  "independent_challenges": [
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-001",
      "challenge": "Could days1 be described as a future midnight deadline, or all day timers invariably invalid?",
      "result": "Rejected both generalizations. D is the value saved alongside t; for t1728000100/day1 its negative value triggers invalid-and-clear. For low epoch t1000/day1 the same formula gives positive86400, so first query87400 fails at equality and87401 can pass. These are manual static countervectors, not new executed model/reference evidence. Candidate states the general saved-duration formula and positivity/strict threshold correctly."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-001",
      "challenge": "Does map refresh itself prove the stored record survives or event pages actually refreshed?",
      "result": "No. Source map refresh traverses event/common-event refresh and script conditions can have effects/failures. Candidate expressly assumes normal refresh with no slot rewrite; receipt counts only the ProbeMap double. No true event-page/save roundtrip claim is made."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-001",
      "challenge": "Would global variable slots or event-private timer storage supply an equivalent existing contract?",
      "result": "No. Game_Variables stores writable slots up to5000; the scoped slot1 is valid. Event timing uses per-event records/self switches and command124 uses play_time. No requirement to expand every ID/malformed-record boundary; bare Ruby arity is outside the architecture-neutral scoped input contract."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-002",
      "challenge": "Could ordinary string-key failure establish every POST input fails or the transport always remains unused?",
      "result": "No. An empty ordinary form statically bypasses all per-pair construction, produces empty body with Content-Length0, and reaches the host once if that host returns200/OK. The symbolk safeASCII historical case also reaches the double. Empty-form control is a static reasoning check only, not run and not newly claimed in candidate. Candidate avoids all-POST/default-game failure claims."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-002",
      "challenge": "Could the recorded string-key FrozenError conceal the encoding error, or convenience output prove transmission?",
      "result": "Yes to concealment; no to transmission. V1 only exposed string-key failures; V2 added symbol keys to isolate ASCII-space ArgumentError. Both source ordering and historic receipts confirm pre-host failure. Candidate separates versions/inputs and labels extended convenience/https controls static."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-002",
      "challenge": "Does absent readable HTTPLite implementation undermine a zero-host-call result?",
      "result": "No for these pre-host cases: stage location and double call counts bound the conclusion before HTTPLite. Actual HTTP engine, network, nonASCII and game runtime remain unverified, as candidate records."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-003",
      "challenge": "Could ordinary uniform random or shuffle explain a single0,0 output, or reset retain a repeat gate?",
      "result": "Single0,0 cannot reject independent uniform. The conditional distribution after first0 differs:1/7 vs1/2. Reset clears the appeared identities before next draw, so no repeat gate on new-round first draw. Candidate states exactly these distinctions."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-003",
      "challenge": "Does the reference random wrapper alter positive-integer range selection or imply cross-language sequence equality?",
      "result": "The inspected positive Numeric path forwards its bound to the native random entry. Uniform integer selection remains an explicit mathematical premise; no native distribution/statistical/cross-language execution is asserted."
    },
    {
      "finding": "GR4-M02-003",
      "challenge": "Do repeated values exhaust unseen state, or can an n1 round get stuck?",
